<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Stress Relief: Best Points" description="Use acupressure for stress relief. Guide to Yintang, HT7, PC6, LV3, and other points that calm the nervous system and reset a frazzled mind." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions/" }, { "label": "Acupressure for Stress Relief", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-stress/" }]} datePublished="2026-08-27" dateModified="2026-08-27" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Stress Relief: Best Points to Calm the Nervous System</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Everyday stress shows up as a tight chest, shallow breathing, and a racing mind. Acupressure offers a quick, drug-free way to interrupt that loop and cue the body toward calm. This guide focuses on short "reset" routines you can use at your desk, in the car, or before sleep — distinct from our longer <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-anxiety/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">anxiety protocol</a>, which is aimed at persistent worry.</p><div class="mb-8 p-4 bg-forest-50 dark:bg-forest-900/20 border-l-4 border-forest-500 rounded-r-lg"><p class="text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><strong>Research Note:</strong> A 2022 systematic review and meta-analysis of acupressure for anxiety (Chen HM et al., J Altern Complement Med, <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35235358/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 underline decoration-forest-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300 dark:hover:text-forest-200">PMID 35235358</a>) found the overall direction of the evidence positive, though the authors call for more high-quality trials. A 2015 review of self-acupressure for symptom management (Song HJ et al., Complement Ther Med, <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25637155/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 underline decoration-forest-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300 dark:hover:text-forest-200">PMID 25637155</a>) reached similar conclusions. The evidence is promising, not proven.</p></div><section id="yintang" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/yintang-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">1. Yintang - Hall of Impression</a></h2><p class="mb-3">Yintang, between the eyebrows, is the fastest "off switch" for a frazzled mind and is often the first point used in a stress reset.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> Midway between the eyebrows, at the bridge of the nose. <a href="/points/yintang-acupressure-point/">Yintang guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press gently with one fingertip and take three slow breaths. Use it anywhere a wave of stress hits.</p></section><section id="ht7" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/ht7-shenmen-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">2. HT7 (Shenmen) - Spirit Gate</a></h2><p class="mb-3">HT7 on the wrist is traditionally used to settle a restless spirit and is a core calming point in stress routines.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> At the wrist crease, on the little-finger side, in the small hollow by the tendon. <a href="/points/ht7-shenmen-acupressure-point/">HT7 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly with your thumb for 1-2 minutes on each wrist.</p></section><section id="pc6" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">3. PC6 (Neiguan) - Inner Gate</a></h2><p class="mb-3">PC6 on the inner forearm is traditionally used to ease the tight, queasy feeling stress can create in the chest and stomach.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> About three finger-widths above the inner wrist crease, between the two forearm tendons. <a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point/">PC6 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly for 1-2 minutes on each arm, breathing slowly.</p></section><section id="lv3" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/lv3-taichong-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">4. LV3 (Taichong) - Great Surge</a></h2><p class="mb-3">LV3 on the foot is traditionally used to move stagnant energy and release irritability and tension held low in the body.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the top of the foot, in the hollow between the first and second toes. <a href="/points/lv3-taichong-acupressure-point/">LV3 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly into the hollow for 1-2 minutes on each foot.</p></section><section id="gv20" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/gv20-baihui-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">5. GV20 (Baihui) - Hundred Meetings</a></h2><p class="mb-3">GV20 at the crown is traditionally used to lift a foggy, overloaded mind and support a sense of clarity.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the midline of the head, about two finger-widths behind the hairline at the top of the skull. <a href="/points/gv20-baihui-acupressure-point/">GV20 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Gentle steady pressure with the fingertips for 1-2 minutes.</p></section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What Is the 2-Minute Stress Reset?</h2><h3 class="mb-3 font-semibold text-lg">Step-by-Step Routine</h3><p class="mb-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Press Yintang and take three slow breaths, then HT7 and PC6 on both sides while you keep breathing evenly, and finish with LV3 on both feet. The whole sequence takes about two minutes and can be repeated whenever stress builds. For ongoing worry that interferes with daily life, see our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-anxiety/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">anxiety guide</a> and consider speaking with a clinician.</p></section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="space-y-4"><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How fast does it work?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">Many people feel a small shift in breathing and tension within a minute or two of pressing Yintang and the wrist points. It is a reset, not a cure, and is best used early and often.</p></details><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is this the same as the anxiety page?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">They overlap on points, but this page targets everyday acute stress and quick resets, while the anxiety guide addresses persistent worry and longer routines.</p></details><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I get help?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">If stress is constant, affects sleep or relationships, or comes with chest pain, breathlessness, or low mood, speak with a healthcare provider. Acupressure is supportive, not a replacement for care.</p></details></div></section><hr class="my-8"><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This content is for informational purposes only and is not medical advice. Consult a licensed healthcare provider for persistent stress or anxiety.</p></div>
  <EvidenceBox refs={["song-2015", "lee-frazier-2011"]} note={"Evidence for acupressure from published reviews."} />
</article>
